{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "squareshare-dashboard",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  // Store dashboard runs on the Cloudflare Workers *Node* runtime via OpenNext.
  // nodejs_compat is required by @opennextjs/cloudflare; the compatibility_date
  // must be >= 2024-09-23 for nodejs_compat to take effect.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-07",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  // CPU per request. The account is on Workers Paid since 2026-10-28; on the
  // Free plan every request got 10 ms, which no page render fits in, and the
  // dashboard died with Error 1102 whenever a visitor clicked around. Pinned so
  // a deploy onto a Free account fails loudly ("CPU limits are not supported
  // for the Free plan") instead of quietly shipping a dashboard that dies. The
  // heaviest real request uses under 2 s; 30 s is Paid's default.
  "limits": {
    "cpu_ms": 30000
  },
  // NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars only — these ship to the browser bundle already, so
  // committing them here is no additional exposure. `wrangler deploy` treats
  // this object as authoritative and wipes any dashboard-added vars not
  // listed here, so this is also the fix for vars disappearing after deploy.
  // Secrets (SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, R2 keys, etc.) do
  // NOT go here — set those with `wrangler secret put` / the CF dashboard.
  "vars": {
    // Where the alerts nobody using the app would report go (lib/ops/alert.ts),
    // once mail is on: the company mailbox.
    "OPS_ALERT_EMAIL": "office@squareshare.eu",
    // The address app mail is sent from: the sender Brevo already relays
    // Supabase Auth's mail as. Sending stays OFF until
    // TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_ENABLED is "true" (LAUNCH SWITCHES, step 3).
    "TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_FROM": "info@squareshare.eu",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL": "https://vnyfndqpdllwhvhinjoi.supabase.co",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZSIsInJlZiI6InZueWZuZHFwZGxsd2h2aGluam9pIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJpYXQiOjE3ODMzMDExNTYsImV4cCI6MjA5ODg3NzE1Nn0.y_SUEDhSU6fmx2HU8r-DHe4XolwAo7JevqBTLtn6ebI",
    "NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL": "https://dashboard.squareshare.eu",
    // Passkeys (lib/auth/passkeys.ts): the domain every passkey is bound to.
    // Public by nature (the browser shows it). The parent domain, so a
    // passkey survives a move between subdomains. NEVER CHANGE IT once
    // passkeys exist: every one registered would stop working. Its sealing
    // key is a secret: `wrangler secret put MFA_PASSKEY_KEY`.
    "WEBAUTHN_RP_ID": "squareshare.eu",
    // The MCP server at /mcp and its OAuth endpoints (lib/agent/access.ts). The
    // kill switch: "false" answers 404 on all of them and touches no grant.
    // Its signing key is a secret: `wrangler secret put AGENT_JWT_SIGNING_KEY`,
    // a private JWK imported into Supabase as a STANDBY signing key, so Auth
    // never signs with it and revoking it there stops agent access alone.
    "AGENT_ACCESS_ENABLED": "true"
    // NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY does NOT belong here, ever. The forms
    // that render the widget are client components, so the site key is
    // inlined at BUILD time and has to be in .env.production; a var in this
    // object only reaches the server at runtime. See LAUNCH SWITCHES below
    // for the order. Until then the bot check is off in production, same as
    // it is in local dev.
  }
  // CONTENT MODERATION needs no binding. The automatic check
  // (lib/moderation/auto) calls its classifier over HTTPS with a secret:
  // `wrangler secret put ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. Without it nothing a seller saves
  // can be approved once migration 20261012_auto_moderation is applied, so set
  // the secret BEFORE that migration. The sweep that settles what no save did
  // is .github/workflows/moderation-sweep.yml, behind CRON_SECRET.
  // LAUNCH SWITCHES, IN ORDER. Everything described below ships OFF. The order
  // matters, and two of the steps lock people out when taken out of turn.
  // `wrangler deploy` wipes any var not listed in `vars` above, so a var set in
  // the dashboard lasts until the next deploy: add it HERE.
  //
  //   1. Secrets (`wrangler secret put <NAME>`). None changes behaviour alone:
  //        ORDER_LINK_SECRET         the buyer's order links. Without it no
  //                                  checkout provider is offered at all
  //                                  (lib/checkout/availability.ts).
  //        MFA_PASSKEY_KEY           passkeys, unavailable without it.
  //        BREVO_SMTP_USER, BREVO_SMTP_KEY, CONTACT_VERIFICATION_KEY
  //                                  see CONTACT VERIFICATION below.
  //        ANALYTICS_VISITOR_SALT, MODERATION_REPORTER_SALT
  //                                  optional, see .env.example.
  //   2. The bot check. Add NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY to .env.production
  //      and deploy, THEN `wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY`. Secret
  //      first means the server demands a token no browser was asked to
  //      solve, and every sign-up fails.
  //   3. Mail: TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_FROM and TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_ENABLED in
  //      `vars` above. THIS ONE CHANGES WHO CAN SELL. From that deploy a
  //      proven contact email is a condition of publishing, so every seller
  //      who has not confirmed theirs has their product pages answer 404
  //      until they do (lib/products/purchasable.ts). Count them first
  //      (profiles with seller_email set and seller_email_verified_at null)
  //      and tell them.
  //      MAIL GOES OVER BREVO'S SMTP RELAY (lib/email/smtp.ts), not its REST
  //      API: the account's Authorised IPs lock refuses every address a Worker
  //      could send from, and the relay's login can only send. So the lock
  //      stays on. Before the var: BREVO_SMTP_USER and BREVO_SMTP_KEY are
  //      Worker secrets, and `POST /api/ops/mail-test` with the ops token
  //      (ERROR_REPORT_TOKEN) sends one test email to OPS_ALERT_EMAIL
  //      whatever the switch says: it must say {"sent":true} first.
  //   4. Buyer payments (Stripe Connect) need 1, 2 and 3: the checkout gate
  //      reads all of them (lib/checkout/availability.ts). See BUYER PAYMENTS
  //      below for its own secrets and switch.
  //   5. `pnpm check:schema` passes before every deploy that carries a
  //      migration (the workflow runs it once SUPABASE_DB_URL is a repo
  //      secret).
  //
  // CONTACT VERIFICATION: a code emailed or texted to the seller's buyer-facing
  // contact email and phone, typed back to prove they own them (see
  // lib/contact-verification). Mail and texts go out through Brevo's REST API
  // (lib/email/send.ts, lib/sms/send.ts), the same account and verified sender
  // domain that already relays Supabase Auth's mail.
  //
  // OFF until switched on, and switching it on is deliberate:
  //   Secrets (`wrangler secret put <NAME>`, never in this file):
  //     BREVO_SMTP_USER, BREVO_SMTP_KEY
  //                               Brevo's SMTP login (the relay that already
  //                               carries Supabase Auth's mail). Email only:
  //                               SMS needs the REST key, BREVO_API_KEY, and
  //                               that API is locked to listed IPs.
  //     CONTACT_VERIFICATION_KEY  32 random bytes, base64url: the HMAC key
  //                               codes are stored under. Rotating it only
  //                               voids codes in flight (15 minutes' worth).
  //   Email proof, vars:
  //     TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_FROM     a sender verified in Brevo
  //     TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_ENABLED  "true"
  //   Phone proof, vars (plus SMS credit on the Brevo account):
  //     SMS_SENDER   up to 11 letters/digits, e.g. "Squareshare"
  //     SMS_ENABLED  "true"
  //
  // Turning EMAIL on makes a proven contact email a condition of publishing,
  // for every seller (grandfathered proofs were cleared by
  // 20260926_contact_verification). With TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_* set but
  // CONTACT_VERIFICATION_KEY missing, nobody can publish and the logs say
  // why: a missing secret must never quietly switch the requirement off.
  // Phones are never required; an unproven one is simply not shown to buyers.
  //
  // SELLER PLANS (Stripe Billing on the platform account, lib/billing).
  // Every account is on Free until both secrets are set; with them, the
  // pricing modal's upgrades go live:
  //   Secrets (`wrangler secret put <NAME>`, never in this file):
  //     STRIPE_SECRET_KEY      a RESTRICTED key (rk_live_...): write Customers,
  //                            Checkout Sessions, Customer Portal, Subscriptions;
  //                            read Prices
  //     STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET  signing secret of the endpoint at
  //                            https://<host>/api/billing/webhook
  // The Stripe side (Prices with the lookup keys in lib/billing/plans.ts,
  // Stripe Tax, the Customer Portal) is set up first; a price that does not
  // match plans.ts refuses checkout rather than charge it.
  //
  // BUYER PAYMENTS (Stripe Connect, lib/payments/connect and
  // lib/checkout/stripe-provider.ts). A buyer pays the SELLER: a direct charge
  // on the seller's own connected (Express) account, our cut as an application
  // fee. OFF until all of these are true, in this order:
  //   a. Connect is enabled on the Stripe platform account, with Express
  //      accounts and the platform's branding and terms filled in.
  //   b. The migration 20261008_seller_payment_accounts is applied
  //      (`pnpm check:schema` says so).
  //   c. Secrets (`wrangler secret put <NAME>`, never in this file):
  //        STRIPE_SECRET_KEY              the same key seller plans use. For
  //                                       Connect it needs, in addition, write
  //                                       access to Connect Accounts, Account
  //                                       Links, Checkout Sessions and Refunds,
  //                                       and read access to Balance, Payouts
  //                                       and Balance Transactions.
  //        STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET  signing secret of a SECOND endpoint,
  //                                       https://<host>/api/payments/webhook,
  //                                       created with "Listen to events on
  //                                       Connected accounts" and subscribed to
  //                                       CONNECT_EVENT_TYPES
  //                                       (lib/payments/connect/webhook.ts).
  //   d. NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_CONNECT_ENABLED="true" in .env.production (it is
  //      read by client components, so it is a build-time value), then deploy.
  //   e. Launch switches 1, 2 and 3 above: a real payment is never taken
  //      without order links, the bot check and working mail.
  // Optional: OPS_ALERT_EMAIL in `vars` above, a mailbox for the alerts nobody
  // using the app would report (a payment that matches no order,
  // lib/ops/alert.ts). They are always logged with an "[ops alert]" prefix.
  // The fee rates in lib/billing/plans.ts are still marked DRAFT: settle them
  // before step d, because every sale from then on is charged one.
  //
  // STOREFRONT EMBED (the storefront on a seller's own website: public/widget.js
  // and the frame at /e/[key]/[...sites]; see src/lib/storefront/embed-frame.ts).
  // OFF until it has a host of its own: without NEXT_PUBLIC_EMBED_URL the
  // snippet cannot be copied, the frame answers 404 and the rule that lets it
  // be framed is not emitted. In this order:
  //   a. DNS: a PROXIED record for embed.squareshare.eu (AAAA 100:: is the
  //      usual placeholder; the Worker answers, nothing is ever sent there).
  //   b. Worker ROUTES for this Worker. DONE 2026-10-10, through the API:
  //        embed.squareshare.eu/widget.js
  //        embed.squareshare.eu/e/*
  //        embed.squareshare.eu/_next/static/*
  //      Routes, not a Custom Domain (which is how dashboard.squareshare.eu is
  //      attached): a custom domain would serve the whole dashboard on the
  //      embed's hostname. They live in Cloudflare, not as `routes` in this
  //      file: declared here, every deploy would replace them with exactly
  //      what is listed.
  //   c. The R2 bucket's CORS allowlist gains https://embed.squareshare.eu
  //      (GET only), or a typeface a seller uploaded falls back inside the
  //      embed. DONE 2026-10-10.
  //   d. NEXT_PUBLIC_EMBED_URL="https://embed.squareshare.eu" in
  //      .env.production (read by client components AND by next.config.ts,
  //      so it is a build-time value), then deploy.
  //   e. Check production, before telling anyone:
  //        curl -sI https://embed.squareshare.eu/e/<any uuid>/example.com
  //          must show  content-security-policy: frame-ancestors example.com
  //          (the literal text ":s1" there means the worker did not
  //          substitute it, and every embed is blank)
  //        curl -sI https://embed.squareshare.eu/e/<any uuid>/www-example-com.filesusr.com/example.com
  //          must show  frame-ancestors www-example-com.filesusr.com example.com
  //          (a site builder's frame above the embed, as Wix makes one)
  //        curl -sI https://dashboard.squareshare.eu/e/<any uuid>/example.com
  //          must show x-frame-options: DENY and NO content-security-policy
  // To take the embed down again, remove the variable and deploy.
  //
  // SIGN-UP GATE (src/lib/auth/signup-intent.ts). Supabase's own sign-up
  // endpoint answers anyone holding the public anon key, so the app's sign-up
  // guards only bind once GoTrue refuses email sign-ups the app did not
  // approve. Migration 20261021_signup_intents is APPLIED (2026-10-21); the
  // hook is OFF until this build is live. In this order:
  //   a. Deploy a build that writes signup intents (any build from 2026-10-21).
  //   b. Supabase Auth > Hooks > Before User Created: Postgres function
  //      public.signup_intent_gate (Management API PATCH config/auth:
  //      hook_before_user_created_enabled=true,
  //      hook_before_user_created_uri=
  //      "pg-functions://postgres/public/signup_intent_gate").
  //   c. Sign up once with a fresh address on production: the confirmation
  //      mail must arrive. Then a direct POST to /auth/v1/signup with the anon
  //      key must answer 403 "Sign up on Square Share."
  // Switching the hook off is the kill switch. Google sign-up is never gated.
  //
  // KNOWING WHEN IT BREAKS. Every unhandled server error is one JSON log line
  // behind "[server error]" (src/instrumentation.ts), and the alerts nobody
  // would report are behind "[ops alert]": point a Workers Logs alert at both
  // prefixes. Optional, to send them somewhere a person looks:
  //   ERROR_REPORT_URL    a collector that takes a JSON POST
  //   ERROR_REPORT_TOKEN  its bearer token, if any
  // Both are Worker SECRETS since 2026-10-21, pointing at /report on the
  // squareshare-uptime Worker (ops/uptime), which emails each error, at
  // most one every 15 minutes. The same Worker checks every public surface
  // once a minute and emails when one is down and when it is back.
  // BACKUPS: the Supabase project is on the Free plan, which keeps none. See
  // .github/workflows/backup.yml for the nightly stopgap and what it needs.
  //
  // ACCOUNT DELETIONS (src/lib/account-deletion). A request made in Settings
  // is carried out by a scheduled job 14 days later, once the seller owes no
  // buyer anything. OFF until one secret exists in two places:
  //   CRON_SECRET   `wrangler secret put CRON_SECRET` (32+ random characters),
  //                 and the same value as a repository secret, which
  //                 .github/workflows/scheduled.yml calls the job with daily.
  // Without it the job's route answers 404 and requests simply wait. The
  // migration 20261010_account_deletion_requests must be applied first.
  //
  // STOREFRONT FUNNEL (src/lib/analytics/funnel). The steps a buyer takes on
  // the way to paying are written to Workers Analytics Engine, and a daily job
  // stores each finished day's counts in Supabase, where the analytics page
  // reads them. OFF until the binding below exists: without it nothing is
  // written and nothing fails. In this order:
  //   a. Analytics Engine is enabled on the Cloudflare account (once, in the
  //      dashboard: Workers & Pages > Analytics Engine). A deploy that carries
  //      the binding before that is refused.
  //   b. The migration 20261012_storefront_funnel_daily is applied
  //      (`pnpm check:schema` says so).
  //   c. Add this beside "ai" above, and deploy:
  //        "analytics_engine_datasets": [
  //          { "binding": "FUNNEL_EVENTS", "dataset": "storefront_funnel" }
  //        ]
  //      The two names are FUNNEL_BINDING and FUNNEL_DATASET in
  //      src/lib/analytics/funnel/record.ts.
  //   d. For the daily job: CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID in `vars` above (32 hex
  //      characters, not a secret), and
  //      `wrangler secret put FUNNEL_ANALYTICS_TOKEN`: an API token with
  //      Account > Account Analytics > Read and nothing else. The job also
  //      needs CRON_SECRET (see ACCOUNT DELETIONS above);
  //      .github/workflows/scheduled.yml calls it daily.
  // Analytics Engine keeps 90 days, and the job rolls the last three finished
  // days on every run, so it can be down for two nights without losing one.
  //
  // Bindings (KV/R2/D1/Hyperdrive/secrets) get added here as the dashboard
  // grows. Secrets for the Workers runtime go in `.dev.vars` (local) and
  // `wrangler secret put` / the CF dashboard (production) — never in this file.
}
